# Resolution

Fixed. The finding is valid even though it affects an explicitly invoked AWS
oracle rather than the production server. The oracle carries real AWS test
credentials, so a caller-supplied plaintext endpoint must be rejected rather
than treated as a supported custom endpoint.

Fix commit: [`811b808f`](https://github.com/justincormack/argmin/commit/811b808f981c3293d75291377733910606199703)
(`tests: require TLS for STS oracle endpoints`).

The Rust oracle now parses and requires HTTPS URLs for both
`S3_TEST_STS_ENDPOINT` and `S3_TEST_S3_CONTROL_ENDPOINT` before reading the
credential variables into its signer. The shell wrapper independently rejects
plain-HTTP overrides before exporting the oracle credential variables. Unit
coverage rejects HTTP for each endpoint family, and direct wrapper checks prove
both command-line overrides fail before any request is made.

The STS/IAM plan now follows the documented AWS endpoint contract: ordinary S3
may use HTTP or HTTPS, while S3 Control and STS require TLS. The current narrow
local `TagResource`/`UntagResource` routes still inherit the S3 listener's
transport and are explicitly recorded as a temporary compatibility gap to be
removed by the typed service-routing work; no unpinned operation-level HTTP
error was added as a shortcut.

Verification:

- `cargo nextest run -p s3-tests --bin sts_oracle --lib`
- `cargo clippy --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings`
- `shellcheck -x scripts/aws-sts-oracle`
- read-only `./scripts/aws-sts-oracle`
- plaintext STS and S3 Control override rejection checks

# Summary
Introduced: the commit added use of S3_TEST_S3_CONTROL_ENDPOINT for signed S3 Control probes but did not add the same HTTPS validation already present for S3_TEST_STS_ENDPOINT.
This commit adds S3 Control probes to the AWS STS oracle. The existing STS endpoint is explicitly required to start with https:// before primary AWS credentials are used, but the newly introduced S3 Control endpoint is read from the environment and used directly. The wrapper script also accepts and exports --s3-control-endpoint without validating the scheme. Because the probe helper constructs an Authorization header containing the AWS access key ID, signed headers, and SigV4 signature, a misconfigured or attacker-influenced http:// S3 Control endpoint would cause those signed requests to be sent over plaintext. This does not expose the AWS secret key, and the default endpoint is HTTPS, but it can disclose credential metadata and replayable signed requests to a network observer during the SigV4 validity window. This affects an explicitly invoked external test/oracle utility, not the production Argmin S3 server.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm the wrapper accepts or derives `S3_TEST_S3_CONTROL_ENDPOINT` without an HTTPS scheme check.
- [x] Confirm `sts_oracle` validates `S3_TEST_STS_ENDPOINT` but lacks equivalent validation for `S3_TEST_S3_CONTROL_ENDPOINT`.
- [x] Confirm the S3 Control probes use the unvalidated endpoint in signed POST requests.
- [x] Confirm the shared helper places access-key/signature material in the Authorization header and sends it to the supplied URL.
- [x] Dynamically reproduce against the real binary by capturing a SigV4 Authorization header on a plaintext HTTP S3 Control endpoint.
## Report
Validated the finding. I built the real Rust target with `cargo build -p s3-tests --bin sts_oracle` and exercised the actual `target/debug/sts_oracle` binary. Code review of the touched paths shows an asymmetric scheme check: `crates/s3-tests/src/bin/sts_oracle.rs:6747-6751` asserts `S3_TEST_STS_ENDPOINT` starts with `https://`, but `crates/s3-tests/src/bin/sts_oracle.rs:6762-6763` reads `S3_TEST_S3_CONTROL_ENDPOINT` and immediately passes it to `run_cross_service_routing_probes` without an HTTPS assertion. The wrapper similarly accepts/exports `s3_control_endpoint` at `scripts/aws-sts-oracle:103-114` without scheme validation. The probes send POSTs to `format!("{s3_control_endpoint}/")` and related paths at `sts_oracle.rs:6636-6645`, `6678-6687`, and `6724-6733`. The helper constructs a SigV4 Authorization header containing the access key ID and signature at `crates/s3-tests/src/helpers.rs:1901-1904`, then sends it on the provided URL at `helpers.rs:1968-1984`.

Crash attempt: running the binary with `S3_TEST_STS_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1` immediately panicked at `sts_oracle.rs:6748` with `S3_TEST_STS_ENDPOINT must be an HTTPS AWS STS endpoint`, confirming the pre-existing STS guard. Valgrind attempt: valgrind was not installed in the container. Debugger attempt: lldb was available; I set `S3_TEST_S3_CONTROL_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:9999` and broke at `sts_oracle.rs:6763`, proving execution reaches the call to `run_cross_service_routing_probes(&endpoint, &s3_control_endpoint, ...)` after reading the HTTP S3 Control endpoint.

Dynamic reproduction: I created `poc_capture_sts_oracle_http_s3_control.py`, which starts a local HTTPS STS shim and a plaintext HTTP S3 Control shim, then runs the real `sts_oracle` with fake credentials. Output included `routing-sts-query-root: ok` and `routing-s3-control-query-root: ok`, then captured the S3 Control request over HTTP. The capture shows `authorization: AWS4-HMAC-SHA256 Credential=LEAKTESTACCESSKEY/20260715/us-east-1/s3/aws4_request, SignedHeaders=content-type;host;x-amz-account-id;x-amz-content-sha256;x-amz-date, Signature=f51670e147d7ac9d2940b4f2fe4eab152a28b8a6b0ff1263c5c1c7b86b88c698` with body `Action=GetCallerIdentity&Version=2011-06-15`. The process later panics only because the minimal STS shim intentionally returns a success response for a later STS error case; the vulnerable plaintext S3 Control signed request had already been captured.

